- content_for :title, "Curator Guide"

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Illinois Data Bank Curator Guide" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: true}
    %summary
      %h1.idb-section-title Illinois Data Bank Curator Guide
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        This guide describes the current curator-facing interface in Illinois Data Bank.
        It focuses on where work happens in the app and what each screen is used for.
      %p
        Curator access is role-based. When you are signed in with curator access, you can review,
        edit, publish, report on, and troubleshoot records across the application.

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Start From the Dataset Record" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: false}
    %summary
      %h2.idb-section-title Start From the Dataset Record
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        Most curation work starts on a dataset page. Use the main dataset views to inspect
        metadata, review file listings, open notes, and move into publishing or version work.
      %ul
        %li Open a dataset record to see its publication state, DOI details, creators, access settings, notes, and related materials in one place.
        %li Use Edit actions on the dataset to move through the metadata forms and save curator-approved changes.
        %li Use the notes area to capture dataset-specific context that should stay with the record inside the application.

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Review Metadata and Files" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: false}
    %summary
      %h2.idb-section-title Review Metadata and Files
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        Curators should treat the edit screens as the main working interface for metadata and file review.
      %ul
        %li Metadata forms are organized by record sections such as title, description, creators, contributors, funders, keywords, and related materials.
        %li File management lives inside the dataset workflow, where you can inspect uploaded files and confirm that documentation and file organization are understandable from the interface.
        %li Access grants can be managed directly on the dataset so you can confirm who can view or edit a private record before publication.

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Handle Review and Publication" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: false}
    %summary
      %h2.idb-section-title Handle Review and Publication
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        Illinois Data Bank now supports curator-driven publication work directly in the application.
      %ul
        %li Use review-related controls on the dataset record to inspect deposits that need attention before publication.
        %li Use the publish action on the dataset when the record is ready to move from draft to published state.
        %li After publication, return to the dataset page to confirm status changes, DOI display, and delivery tracking details.

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Manage Versions" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: false}
    %summary
      %h2.idb-section-title Manage Versions
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        Versioning tools are attached to the dataset record rather than separated into a legacy workflow.
      %ul
        %li Use version controls to review pending version requests and compare the current record with the requested update.
        %li Approve or reject version requests from the dataset workflow when a depositor submits changes to an existing published record.
        %li Use the version acknowledgement and version history views to confirm what has already been processed.

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Audit Deliveries and Storage Work" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: false}
    %summary
      %h2.idb-section-title Audit Deliveries and Storage Work
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        Curator operations that involve downstream systems are available from the staff tools area.
      %ul
        %li The External Delivery Audit page shows ingest and delivery attempts, response status, replay controls, and unmatched response events.
        %li Dataset pages surface delivery health summaries so you can see whether ingest activity succeeded, failed, or needs follow-up.
        %li The file audit report workflow generates curator reports for storage and file review tasks, with downloadable outputs from the reports area.

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Use Metrics and Reports" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: false}
    %summary
      %h2.idb-section-title Use Metrics and Reports
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        Illinois Data Bank includes built-in reporting screens for curator review and service monitoring.
      %ul
        %li Curator Reports tracks generated operational reports, including file audits.
        %li Metrics pages provide dataset, file, and download reporting for service analysis.
        %li Administrative metrics are available to support higher-level oversight of repository activity.

%section.idb-section-card{aria: { label: "Staff Tools Page" }}
  %details.idb-section-disclosure{open: false}
    %summary
      %h2.idb-section-title Staff Tools Page
    .idb-section-body
      %p
        The Curator and Admin Page is the main staff landing page for operational actions.
      %ul
        %li Use it to open curator reports, delivery audit tools, and other operational screens.
        %li Admin-only controls appear there for service configuration, but curator-facing operational tools are grouped on the same page.
        %li If you need to troubleshoot a dataset, start on the dataset record and then move to reports or delivery audit views as needed.

.idb-workflow-strip.idb-workflow-strip-bottom
  %nav.idb-workflow-actions-right{aria: { label: "Curator guide actions" }}
    = link_to "Curator and Admin Page", admin_path, class: "idb-button-secondary"
